Tramuntana's poverty rate of 42.6 per cent — nearly five times that of Estret — reflects the province's isolation, limited infrastructure, and dependence on subsistence agriculture. Migjorn, despite containing the canal's eastern terminus, has a poverty rate of 28.4 per cent, as the benefits of canal traffic are concentrated in the port facilities and do not extend to the agricultural interior. Jala's poverty rate of 19.2 per cent, while still above the national average, has declined from 24.1 per cent a decade earlier, reflecting the employment generated by the Badia Curta industrial complex. The extreme poverty rate (defined as income below $1.90 per day) is 16.8 per cent in Tramuntana, compared to 1.8 per cent in Estret.

=== Income distribution ===

The distribution of income across the population is highly skewed. The top 10 per cent of earners account for approximately 42 per cent of national income, while the bottom 40 per cent account for approximately 12 per cent. The concentration of wealth at the top is driven by the financial services sector in Sant Cristòfor, the canal-related industries, and the commercial class of the canal corridor, which includes Balboans of all ethnic backgrounds. The middle class — defined as households earning between 80 per cent and 200 per cent of the national median income — constitutes approximately 35 per cent of the population, concentrated in Sant Cristòfor, Sant Bart, and Novara.

The racial dimension of income distribution is complicated by geography. Within Estret Province, ethnic income disparities are relatively modest, as the canal economy has created a multi-ethnic professional class. Outside Estret, however, the disparities are stark: white Balboans in the interior provinces earn more than their Black and Birú neighbours but remain poor in absolute terms, while the Birú population in the remote highland districts is among the most economically marginalised on the isthmus. Approximately 35 per cent of white Balboan households fall within the bottom two income quintiles nationally — a reflection of the community's concentration in the poorer provinces — compared to approximately 55 per cent of Black Balboan households and 80 per cent of Birú households.

=== The Balboa paradox ===

[[File:Estret Tramuntana income comparison chart.png|thumb|250px|Median household income in Estret and Tramuntana provinces]]

{{main|Balboa paradox}}

The persistence of widespread poverty in a country that controls one of the most important commercial waterways on Adonia is a subject of extensive study in development economics and has come to be known as the [[Balboa paradox]]. The term refers to the striking contrast between the wealth generated by the canal and the financial services sector in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and the deep poverty of the interior provinces, where living standards resemble those of low-income countries rather than an upper-middle-income economy.

The paradox is explained by several interrelated factors: the geographic concentration of canal-derived wealth in the Estret corridor, creating an enclave economy that does not diffuse into the interior; the legacy of the seventy-four-year separation (1899–1973) between the canal zone and the rump state, which left the two economies at vastly different levels of development at reunification; the structure of the cooperative farm system, which replaced the planter oligarchy but has proven significantly less productive than the former plantations; and the racial dimension of economic exclusion, whereby the communities historically excluded from the formal economy — Black Balboans and the Birú — have not been fully integrated despite the Guarantees. The paradox has been the subject of political debate since the reunification, with the BPP government pursuing redistribution and infrastructure investment, the Democratic Alliance calling for cooperative farm reform, and the Socialist Front advocating more aggressive redistribution of canal revenue.

== Currency and monetary policy ==

Balboa uses the [[International Dollar]] ($), the shared currency of the [[Adonian Community]], which it adopted immediately upon AC accession in 1973. The previous currency, the [[Balboan peso]], was retired at par with the International Dollar as part of the reunification settlement. Monetary policy is set by the [[AC Central Bank]], which maintains a policy of low inflation and stable exchange rates. The adoption of the International Dollar has facilitated trade and investment by eliminating currency risk, though it has also constrained the government's ability to use monetary policy to respond to economic shocks.

The International Dollar is overvalued for Balboa's economy: it is calibrated for the AC's richer members, and its purchasing power exceeds what a hypothetical independent Balboan currency would command. This overvaluation is the structural cause of the negative tariff regime and the domestic processing chain strategy — it makes imports cheap (benefiting petroleum refining and component assembly) but exports expensive (requiring the negative tariff to make refined and manufactured goods competitive). The overvaluation also suppresses tourism by making Balboa an expensive destination for travellers from countries with weaker currencies.

== Government finances ==

{{main|Budget of Balboa}}

Government revenue is derived from a combination of {{w|income tax}}, {{w|Value added tax|VAT}}, {{w|land value tax}}, {{w|corporate tax}}, {{w|customs}} duties, and other sources. Total general fund revenue for 2026 is estimated at approximately $58.1 billion (13.8% of nominal GDP), against total expenditure of approximately $61.5 billion, producing a deficit of approximately $3.4 billion (0.8% of GDP). The deficit is financed through domestic and international borrowing, within an estimated annual borrowing capacity of $6.5 billion. National debt stands at 28% of GDP ($119 billion), with annual debt service of $5.3 billion (9.1% of revenue).

The [[Balboa Canal]] generates approximately $6.5 billion in annual toll revenue, which is fully earmarked for specific programmes and does not flow into the general fund. Canal revenue funds the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] export compensation regime ($1.1 billion), agricultural price supports and cooperative assistance ($1.2 billion), canal operations and maintenance ($1.5 billion), the [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] (FIDB, $0.3 billion or approximately 5% of canal revenue), other earmarks ($0.4 billion), and debt service ($2.0 billion).

The FIDB, established in 1994, is Balboa's {{w|sovereign wealth fund}}, with assets under management of approximately $65 billion. The FIDB holds strategic stakes in the domestic processing chain — including positions in [[Companyia Química Occidental]] (CQO), [[Grup Rojo]] (GRO), [[Corporació de Plàstics Jala]] (CPJ), and other chemical and industrial companies — and its investments are intended to support the development of the domestic industrial base while generating returns for the national treasury. The fund distributes 2% of its AUM annually to the national budget, yielding approximately $1.3 billion.

The personal income tax is progressive, with a $6,000 annual exemption threshold and rates ranging from 10 per cent to 28 per cent. Approximately 38.9 per cent of the employed workforce pays income tax. The corporate tax rate is 25 per cent, though companies operating in the [[Sant Cristòfor Free Trade Zone]] benefit from reduced rates and other incentives. A {{w|value added tax}} (VAT) of 10 per cent applies to most goods and services, with basic food items and medicines exempt. A national {{w|land value tax}}, established in 1993, taxes the unimproved value of land at rates varying by zone.

